Rivet installs commands without default key mappings. Configure the editor with `require('rivet').setup(options)`.

## Setup options

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `projects_dir` | `nil` | Parent for new folders; unset uses Neovim's working directory. The destination remains editable. Current-folder creation always uses `:pwd`. |
| `task_output` | `'keep'` | Keep completed output visible. `'close-on-success'` closes successful final task output and retains it for `:RivetOutput`; failed or cancelled output stays visible. |
| `default_preset` | `'debug'` | Preferred choice before a project has a remembered selection. C/C++ uses available CMake configure presets; Python, Cargo, SwiftPM, and Lua offer `debug` and `release`. |
| `profiles` | `{}` | Named sets of creation choices. Configured profiles take precedence over saved local profiles with the same name. |
| `identity` | `nil` | Optional file-header defaults. If omitted, the wizard can use an identity explicitly saved with `:RivetIdentity`; otherwise fields start blank. |

For example:

```lua
require('rivet').setup({
  default_preset = 'debug',
  profiles = {
    ['Portable C++'] = {
      language = 'cpp',
      standard = 20,
      modules = false,
      std_modules = false,
      kind = 'app',
      layout = 'feature',
      style = 'LLVM',
      generator = 'Ninja',
    },
    ['C++26 modules'] = {
      language = 'cpp',
      standard = 26,
      modules = true,
      module_layout = 'single',
    },
  },
})
```

Creation profiles prefill editable wizard answers. The C++ `profile` field selects a workload (`general`, `cpu`, `openmp`, `mpi`, `cuda`, `metal`, `webgpu`, `vulkan`, or `opengl`); it is independent of your saved profile's name. See the [CLI options](/docs/cli/) for creation defaults.

## Creation profiles

Run `:RivetProfile` inside a Rivet project, give the profile a name, and approve its preview with `c`. All profiles save `language`; additional fields are:

| Provider | Saved creation choices |
| --- | --- |
| C++ | `standard`, `modules`, `std_modules`, `module_layout`, `kind`, `layout`, `style`, `header_guard`, `generator`, `profile`, and CUDA's `cuda_standard`, `cuda_architectures` when present |
| C | `kind`, `standard`, `header_guard` |
| Python | `kind`, `manager` |
| Rust | `kind`, `edition` |
| Swift | `kind` |
| Lua | `kind`, `runtime` |

Project names, destinations, compiler paths, dependencies, files, Git actions, and identity are excluded. Saved profiles appear in the next creation wizard.

## Header identity

`:RivetIdentity` collects optional details, previews styles, and then asks you to apply the local preference. The creation wizard offers **Use saved identity and preview styles**; you can edit or omit those details for each project. Nothing is inferred from your OS account or Git configuration.

The `identity` setup option accepts `style`, `author`, `email`, `organization`, `created`, `copyright`, `license`, and `description`; see [file comment headers](/docs/comment-headers/).

Saved profiles and identity live in `stdpath('state')/rivet/preferences.json`. Remove a named entry or the `identity` property there to forget it. Existing projects retain their own manifest defaults; `:RivetHeaderDefaults` changes those separately.

## Build choices and status

Configure/build/test presets, the Build target, application, and last task are stored per project under `stdpath('state')/rivet/tasks/`. `:RivetStatus` shows them, and the task picker includes them in its prompt. You may use `require('rivet').statusline()` in your statusline plugin to display the active Rivet project choices.

Creation profiles and CMake presets serve different purposes: profiles start a new project, while CMake presets configure and run builds inside one. See [CMake and tasks](/docs/cmake/) for their commands and selection rules.

## Clang tool configuration

`:RivetConfig` (or `:Rivet config`) edits common clang-format, clang-tidy, and clangd settings. Each entry shows its meaning, type, current value, and choices before a file-diff review. Per-check options accept a `check.option` key. **Open YAML** opens the full configuration, including complex or conditional blocks the guided editor cannot preserve.

```vim
:RivetConfig clang-format
:RivetConfig clang-tidy
:RivetConfig clangd
```

Generated clang-format settings inherit the selected style; clang-tidy enables analyzer, bug-prone, performance, and selected modernize checks; clangd points at the selected compilation database. New C++ files use the current project `.clang-format`.

The editor validates with installed tools when available and reports skipped validation. Accepted values depend on the installed LLVM version. Full references: [clang-format](https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ClangFormatStyleOptions.html), [clang-tidy](https://clang.llvm.org/extra/clang-tidy/), and [clangd](https://clangd.llvm.org/config).

```sh
rivet config clang-format
rivet config clang-format IndentWidth 4 --preview
rivet config clang-tidy Checks '-*,clang-analyzer-*,bugprone-*' --preview
```

[Project upgrades](/docs/upgrading/) preserve locally edited tool configuration.
